  "answer_text": "The complete quotation is:\n\n> **\"...those owned by wealthy rulers.\"**\n\n### Context and Meaning\nThis famous quote is attributed to the 14th-century Arab sociologist, historian, and philosopher **Ibn Khaldun**, written in his seminal 1377 work, ***The Muqaddimah*** (also known as the *Prolegomena*).\n\nIn this text, Ibn Khaldun laid down early foundations for sociology and economics, including insights into free-market dynamics and the division of labor. This quote reflects his strong opposition to state-run commerce:\n\n1. **The Danger of State Monopolies:** Ibn Khaldun argued that when rulers or governments engage in commercial trade, they inevitably abuse their political and military power. They buy goods cheaply through coercion and sell them at artificially high prices, which destroys fair competition and drives private merchants out of business. \n2. **The Efficiency of Private Enterprise:** Private merchants, who do not have the luxury of state backing, must rely on careful organization, financial responsibility, reputation, and customer satisfaction to survive. \n3. **Long-Term Sustainability:** While a ruler's wealth might seem immense, it is tied to the highly unstable political lifecycle of a dynasty. Organised, private merchant-owned businesses\u2014relying on sustainable economic principles\u2014will ultimately outlast and surpass the commercial endeavors of wealthy monarchs.",
